    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "Su cient reagents are provided for approximately 60 assays of 1 mL samples, each containing approximately 1 x 106 cells/mL."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.609, 479.878, 206.003, 503.503 ],
    "font" : "MyriadPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.5,
    "text color" : "[0.431, 1.0, 0.432, 0.277]",
    "content" : "Detecting DNA Strand Breaks with ChromaTide® Nucleotides"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.605, 347.339, 308.609, 480.21 ],
    "font" : "MinionPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 8.8,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "Break sites have traditionally been labeled with biotinylated dUTP, followed by subsequent detection with an avidin or streptavidin conjugate37–40 (Section 7.6, Table 7.9). However, a more direct approach for detecting DNA strand breaks in apoptotic cells is possible via the use of our ChromaTide® BODIPY® FL-14-dUTP (C7614) as a TdT substrate41,42 (Figure 15.5.9).  e single-step BODIPY® FL dye–based assay has several advantages over indirect detection of biotinylated or haptenylated nucleotides, including fewer protocol steps and increased cell yields. BODIPY® FL dye–labeled nucleotides have also proven superior to  uorescein-labeled nucleotides for detection of DNA strand breaks in apoptotic cells because they provide stronger signals, a narrower emission spectrum and less photobleaching41 (Figure 15.5.9)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.609, 303.339, 308.611, 348.324 ],
    "font" : "MinionPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 8.8,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "In situ DNA modi cations by labeled nucleotides have been used to detect DNA fragmentation in what may be apoptotic cells in autopsy brains of Huntingtons and Alzheimer disease patients.43–46 DNA fragmentation is also associated with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.47"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 35,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 322.609, 633.339, 575.627, 711.324 ],
    "font" : "MinionPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 8.8,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "Analogous to TdT’s ability to label double-strand breaks, the E. coli repair enzyme DNA polymerase I can be used to detect single-strand nicks,48,49 which appear as a relatively early step in some apoptotic processes.50–52 Because our ChromaTide® BODIPY® FL-14-dUTP (C7614) and ChromaTide®  uorescein-12-dUTP53,54 (C7604) are incorporated into DNA by E. coli DNA polymerase I, they are also e ective for in situ labeling with the nick translation method.55"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 322.609, 611.882, 562.471, 624.502 ],
    "font" : "MyriadPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.5,
    "text color" : "[0.431, 1.0, 0.432, 0.277]",
    "content" : "High-Content Analysis of Genotoxicity and Cytotoxicity"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 37,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 322.609, 556.339, 575.609, 612.21 ],
    "font" : "MinionPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 8.8,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "In mammalian cells, a double-strand break (DSB) in genomic DNA is a potentially lethal lesion. One of the earliest known responses to DSB formation is phosphorylation of H2A histones. Speci cally, DNA damaging agents induce phosphorylation of histone variant H2AX at Ser139, leading to the formation of DNA foci at the site of DNA DSBs.56–58"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 38,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 322.605, 479.339, 575.605, 557.21 ],
    "font" : "MinionPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 8.8,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : " e HCS DNA Damage Kit (H10292) was developed to enable simultaneous quantitation of two cell health parameters, genotoxicity and cytotoxicity, by high-content analysis in the same cell (Figure 15.5.10). DNA damage is measured as an indication of genotoxicity and accomplished by speci c antibody-based detection of phosphorylated H2AX (Ser139) in the nucleus. Cytotoxicity is measured with the Image-iT® DEAD Green™ viability stain (also available as a stand-alone reagent, I10291),"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 358.761, 351.986, 539.457, 463.041 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_66_pg1_images/imageFile1.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "caption",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Caption",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 323.609, 303.963, 575.624, 345.844 ],
    "linked content id" : 17,
    "font" : "MyriadPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 7.0,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "Figure 15.5.9 HL-60 cells treated with camptothecin for 3 hours. The DNA strand nicks characteristic of apoptosis were detected with the TUNEL (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase–mediated dUTP nick end-labeling) assay using the  uorescently labeled nucleotide ChromaTide® BODIPY® FL-14-dUTP (C7614). Image contributed by Zbigniew Darzynkiewicz, Cancer Research Institute, New York Medical College."
  }, {
